"52_collection_ext.mst — the common Collection protocol a probe found missing,
 all pure Smalltalk on the existing classes (Object>Collection>Sequenceable
 Collection>ArrayedCollection>Array/String, and OrderedCollection/Interval).
 Reopens Collection and SequenceableCollection to ADD methods (the world/40
 reopen idiom), built on do:/inject:into:/isEmpty/includes:/at:/size/
 copyFrom:to:/reversed/at:put:/asSortedCollection — all of which already work.

 Fills: sum/max/min/average, includesAll:, ifEmpty:/ifNotEmpty: family,
 first:/last:/allButFirst.../second..fourth, copy, reverse, sort/sorted."

Object subclass: Collection [
    "Reductions. Empty is an error (there is no meaningful identity for max/min,
     and matching Pharo, sum of empty is refused rather than assumed 0)."
    sum ^ <Number> [
        | total any |
        any := false. total := 0.
        self do: [:e | any ifTrue: [ total := total + e ] ifFalse: [ total := e. any := true ]].
        any ifFalse: [ ^self error: 'sum of an empty collection' ].
        ^total
    ]
    max ^ <Object> [
        | best any |
        any := false. best := nil.
        self do: [:e | any ifTrue: [ e > best ifTrue: [ best := e ] ] ifFalse: [ best := e. any := true ]].
        any ifFalse: [ ^self error: 'max of an empty collection' ].
        ^best
    ]
    min ^ <Object> [
        | best any |
        any := false. best := nil.
        self do: [:e | any ifTrue: [ e < best ifTrue: [ best := e ] ] ifFalse: [ best := e. any := true ]].
        any ifFalse: [ ^self error: 'min of an empty collection' ].
        ^best
    ]
    average ^ <Number> [ ^self sum / self size ]

    includesAll: aCollection <Collection> ^ <Boolean> [
        aCollection do: [:e | (self includes: e) ifFalse: [ ^false ] ].
        ^true
    ]
    includesAny: aCollection <Collection> ^ <Boolean> [
        aCollection do: [:e | (self includes: e) ifTrue: [ ^true ] ].
        ^false
    ]

    "Empty-guards. The block is evaluated with no argument (the common
     `coll ifNotEmpty: [ ... ]` form)."
    ifEmpty: aBlock <[]> ^ <Object> [ ^self isEmpty ifTrue: [ aBlock value ] ifFalse: [ self ] ]
    ifNotEmpty: aBlock <[]> ^ <Object> [ ^self isEmpty ifTrue: [ nil ] ifFalse: [ aBlock value ] ]
    ifEmpty: emptyBlock <[]> ifNotEmpty: notEmptyBlock <[]> ^ <Object> [
        ^self isEmpty ifTrue: [ emptyBlock value ] ifFalse: [ notEmptyBlock value ]
    ]
    ifNotEmpty: notEmptyBlock <[]> ifEmpty: emptyBlock <[]> ^ <Object> [
        ^self isEmpty ifTrue: [ emptyBlock value ] ifFalse: [ notEmptyBlock value ]
    ]
]

Object subclass: Collection [
    "The `withAll:` constructor: a new instance holding every element of
     aCollection. Works for any GROWABLE collection (OrderedCollection/Set/Bag
     — `new` gives an empty one and `addAll:` fills it); Array overrides below
     because a fixed-size array can't grow."
    Collection class >> withAll: aCollection <Collection> ^ <Self> [
        ^self new addAll: aCollection; yourself
    ]

    "The `with:` family — the everyday literal constructor (`Set with: 1
     with: 2`). Array and String define their own on the fixed-size shape;
     these are the growable ones, and until now they did not exist AT ALL.
     Nobody noticed because a missing class-side selector used to answer nil
     in silence rather than raising doesNotUnderstand, so `OrderedCollection
     with: 3` quietly evaluated to nil for as long as the class has existed.
     The dNU hole is closed; these are the methods it was hiding."
    Collection class >> with: a <Object> ^ <Self> [
        ^self new add: a; yourself
    ]
    Collection class >> with: a <Object> with: b <Object> ^ <Self> [
        ^self new add: a; add: b; yourself
    ]
    Collection class >> with: a <Object> with: b <Object> with: c <Object> ^ <Self> [
        ^self new add: a; add: b; add: c; yourself
    ]
    Collection class >> with: a <Object> with: b <Object> with: c <Object>
                       with: d <Object> ^ <Self> [
        ^self new add: a; add: b; add: c; add: d; yourself
    ]
]

ArrayedCollection subclass: Array [
    Array class >> withAll: aCollection <Collection> ^ <Array> [
        | arr i |
        arr := self new: aCollection size.
        i := 0.
        aCollection do: [:e | i := i + 1. arr at: i put: e ].
        ^arr
    ]

    "ANSI class-side constructors Dolphin's control code sends. Both are
     reached from inside a WM_NOTIFY handler — `new:withAll:` from the
     TreeView's indent handling, `writeStream:` from a list item's
     `displayString` — and that handler contains its errors by design, so a
     miss here never surfaces as a failed assertion, only as a `handler error
     in ...>>wmNotify:` line.

     THESE COULD NOT WORK UNTIL DD11. `Array` is a bridged core name: a world
     file's reopen is holder-ized to `Array ext`, so its class-side methods
     land on `Array ext class`, and no dispatch path looked there. `withAll:`
     above had been dead since the day it was written. The fix is in
     `STSendCommon`'s class-side fallback (port-win/dart_st/st_natives.cc)."
    Array class >> new: anInteger <Integer> withAll: anObject <Object> ^ <Array> [
        | r |
        r := self new: anInteger.
        1 to: anInteger do: [ :i | r at: i put: anObject ].
        ^r
    ]
    Array class >> writeStream: anInteger <Integer> ^ <WriteStream> [
        ^WriteStream on: (self new: anInteger)
    ]
    Array class >> writeStream ^ <WriteStream> [
        ^self writeStream: 0
    ]
]

Collection subclass: SequenceableCollection [
    "Prefix / suffix slices (copyFrom:to: clamps an empty range to an empty
     result, so the edge cases fall out for free)."
    first: n <Integer> ^ <SequenceableCollection> [ ^self copyFrom: 1 to: n ]
    last: n <Integer> ^ <SequenceableCollection> [ ^self copyFrom: self size - n + 1 to: self size ]
    allButFirst ^ <SequenceableCollection> [ ^self copyFrom: 2 to: self size ]
    allButFirst: n <Integer> ^ <SequenceableCollection> [ ^self copyFrom: n + 1 to: self size ]
    allButLast ^ <SequenceableCollection> [ ^self copyFrom: 1 to: self size - 1 ]
    allButLast: n <Integer> ^ <SequenceableCollection> [ ^self copyFrom: 1 to: self size - n ]

    second ^ <Object> [ ^self at: 2 ]
    third ^ <Object> [ ^self at: 3 ]
    fourth ^ <Object> [ ^self at: 4 ]

    "NOTE: no `copy` here. It used to be `^self copyFrom: 1 to: self size`,
     which worked for Array/String but DNU'd on OrderedCollection (whose
     copyFrom:to: builds via at:put:) and errored on Symbol ('symbols are
     immutable'). world/59 supplies the real one — Object>>copy =
     shallowCopy + postCopy, on the shallowCopy primitive — which covers
     every sequence correctly, including those two."
    reverse ^ <SequenceableCollection> [ ^self reversed ]

    "Non-destructive sorts return a fresh Array; sort/sort: order the
     receiver IN PLACE (via at:put:) and answer it."
    sorted ^ <Array> [ ^self asSortedCollection asArray ]
    sorted: aSortBlock <[Object,Object,^Boolean]> ^ <Array> [ ^(self asSortedCollection: aSortBlock) asArray ]
    sort ^ <Self> [
        | ordered |
        ordered := self sorted.
        1 to: self size do: [:i | self at: i put: (ordered at: i) ].
        ^self
    ]
    sort: aSortBlock <[Object,Object,^Boolean]> ^ <Self> [
        | ordered |
        ordered := self sorted: aSortBlock.
        1 to: self size do: [:i | self at: i put: (ordered at: i) ].
        ^self
    ]
]

"── `new:` on the GROWABLE collections ──────────────────────────────────────

 `OrderedCollection new: 12` answered the CLASS. So did `Set new: 4`,
 `Dictionary new: 4`, and every other growable collection: none of them
 defined a class-side `new:`, so the send fell through to
 `Behavior>>new: n [ ^self basicNew: n ]`, and `basicNew:` on a class that is
 not INDEXABLE answers the class itself rather than failing.

 That is a silent wrong answer of the worst kind — the result is an object, it
 responds to sends, and it is simply not the thing that was asked for. It
 surfaced three layers away inside Dolphin's command routing, as
 `Class '_Type' has no instance method 'identityIncludes_'`, because
 `CommandPolicy>>routeFrom:` opens with `path := OrderedCollection new: 12`.
 Every command route in the port had been building on a class.

 IN DOLPHIN AND ANSI, the argument is a CAPACITY HINT, not a size: `new: 12`
 answers an EMPTY collection with room for twelve. So forwarding to `new` is
 correct behaviour, not an approximation — these implementations grow on
 demand, so the hint has nothing to buy.

 DEFINED PER CLASS, NOT ON `Collection`. Array and String are Collections too,
 and theirs is INDEXABLE `new:` — n slots, handled by the VM through
 `basicNew:`. A definition on `Collection` would sit between them and
 `Behavior`, and would have broken `Array new: 4` while fixing the others."
OrderedCollection class extend [ new: anInteger <Integer> ^ <Self> [ ^self new ] ]
Set class extend [ new: anInteger <Integer> ^ <Self> [ ^self new ] ]
Bag class extend [ new: anInteger <Integer> ^ <Self> [ ^self new ] ]
Dictionary class extend [ new: anInteger <Integer> ^ <Self> [ ^self new ] ]
